{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Rich curd of cattle.</b> The choicest of meat." "<b>And the fat of sheep.</b> Were it not for the received tradition, this verse would be a strong proof regarding ḥelev, as I mentioned in its place. Or the sense of חלב כרים is the rich, good fat, like כל חלב יצהר (all the fat of oil)." "<b>Rams of Bashan.</b> That grew fat on the mountain of Bashan." "<b>The fat of kidneys of wheat.</b> By way of parable, for the fat is upon the kidneys, and also the grain of wheat resembles one of the kidneys." "<b>And the blood of grapes.</b> The wine. And behold it abounds in honey and oil and meat and bread and wine and every fruit, and this is תנובות שדי (the yield of Shaddai). And some say that דבש is date honey." "<b>Wine.</b> Like יין (wine), and so כרם חמר (vineyard of wine)." "The letter vav of <b>תשתה</b> (you drink) is punctuated with pataḥ, and the sense is: like one speaking to his companion — so great is the abundance that you drink wine at all times. And some say that ענב is feminine, but this is nothing, for the vav should have had a pataḥ until it was pointed to others. And some say it is according to the language of the congregation, for Israel is both masculine and feminine, and this is somewhat close."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {6 "The clause 'until it was pointed to others' (עד שיצא לאחרים) is obscure; translated hyper-literally. Ibn Ezra appears to be arguing about the vocalization of the vav conjugation."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:25:59.482285302Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}